Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A Short History of Chinese Philosophy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Traces Chinese thought from its ancient origins through the twentieth century, focusing on how different schools addressed human nature, morality, social order, politics, and the relationship between humanity and the natural world. It surveys the “Hundred Schools” of the classical period—including Confucianism, Daoism, Mohism, the School of Names, Yin-Yang theory, and Legalism—before examining the development of Confucian metaphysics, the influence of Buddhism and Chan (Zen), the emergence of Neo-Confucianism, and the eventual encounter with Western philosophy. Throughout, Fung presents Confucianism and Daoism as the central currents of Chinese philosophy while emphasizing that Chinese thinkers generally valued practical wisdom, ethical cultivation, harmony, and the art of living over abstract speculation alone.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a good work to understand roughly the nature of Chinese philosophy through history.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><h5>Meta &amp; Misc.</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Chinese philosophy isn’t concerned with gaining positive knowledge, but rather, spiritual elevation\">Chinese philosophy isn’t concerned with gaining positive knowledge, but rather, spiritual elevation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Merchants were the lowest social class in Imperial China\">Merchants were the lowest social class in Imperial China</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Chinese philosophy is inseparable from political thought\">Chinese philosophy is inseparable from political thought</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Civilizational continuity, for the Chinese, requires cultural continuance, not political continuance\">Civilizational continuity, for the Chinese, requires cultural continuance, not political continuance</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suggestiveness, not articulateness, is the ideal of Chinese art\">Suggestiveness, not articulateness, is the ideal of Chinese art</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A distinction in meaning is only extant given some actual change in the way we act\">A distinction in meaning is only extant given some actual change in the way we act</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China’s defeat in the Sino-Japanese war undermined Chinese belief in their cultural superiority\">China’s defeat in the Sino-Japanese war undermined Chinese belief in their cultural superiority</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The paramount contribution of Western philosophy to Chinese philosophy is that of positive logical analysis\">The paramount contribution of Western philosophy to Chinese philosophy is that of positive logical analysis</a></li></ul><p>There are three types of concepts by intuition:</p><ol><li>Differentiated aesthetic continuum,</li><li>Undifferentiated aesthetic continuum, and</li><li>Concepts of the differentiations.</li></ol><p>It is argued that Confucianism is characterized by a study of the concrete concepts of the differentiations, whereas Taoism is characterized by a study of the undifferentiated aesthetic continuum.</p><h5>Confucianism</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucius reinterpreted existing moral teachings\">Confucius reinterpreted existing moral teachings</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucius emphasized righteousness (义) and human-heartedness (仁)\">Confucius emphasized righteousness (义) and human-heartedness (仁)</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism operates upon categorical imperatives\">Confucianism operates upon categorical imperatives</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance\">Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The state is legitimized when it acts as a moral institution, and its head, a moral leader\">The state is legitimized when it acts as a moral institution, and its head, a moral leader</a></li><li>Mencius: <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately good\">All humans are innately good</a></li><li>Xunzi: <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately evil\">All humans are innately evil</a><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Morality exists in order to apportion satisfaction\">Morality exists in order to apportion satisfaction</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rituals help us manage scarcity, process emotions, form group bonds\">Rituals help us manage scarcity, process emotions, form group bonds</a></li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To reach enlightenment, things must be done sincerely, understanding, and intentionality\">To reach enlightenment, things must be done sincerely, understanding, and intentionality</a></li></ul><p><strong>Neo-Confucianism</strong></p><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All things are one and the same\">All things are one and the same</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For the sage, emotions are external\">For the sage, emotions are external</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One should act according to one’s natural abilities\">One should act according to one’s natural abilities</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately good\">All humans are innately good</a></li><li>The School of Principle: <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One receives external moral truths\">One receives external moral truths</a><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"There exist eternal and perfect metaphysical frameworks of physical things\">There exist eternal and perfect metaphysical frameworks of physical things</a></li><li>Zhu Xi has an easier time explaining objective standards independent of individual judgment, but a harder time explaining how we gain reliable access to them.</li></ul></li><li>The School of Mind: <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Moral truths arise internally and naturally\">Moral truths arise internally and naturally</a><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To reach enlightenment, things must be done sincerely, understanding, and intentionality\">To reach enlightenment, things must be done sincerely, understanding, and intentionality</a></li><li>Wang makes moral knowledge immediately accessible in principle, but then has a harder time explaining how we can independently verify that what feels like <em>liangzhi</em> really is <em>liangzhi</em> rather than prejudice, desire, or self-deception.</li></ul></li></ul><h5>Mohism</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Mohists were mainly derived from the lower classes of society\">Mohists were mainly derived from the lower classes of society</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Everyone in the world should love everyone else without discrimination\">Everyone in the world should love everyone else without discrimination</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Only an absolute monarch can keep people out of a ‘state of nature’\">Only an absolute monarch can keep people out of a ‘state of nature’</a></li></ul><h5>Taoism</h5><p><strong>Yang Zhu</strong></p><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One has no other obligations except to oneself\">One has no other obligations except to oneself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One ought to be useless\">One ought to be useless</a></li></ul><p><strong>Laozi</strong></p><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To achieve anything, one must begin with the opposite\">To achieve anything, one must begin with the opposite</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should desire less\">We should desire less</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The state should do as little as possible\">The state should do as little as possible</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One should strive to overcome emotion with reason\">One should strive to overcome emotion with reason</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"By becoming one with the cosmos, one finds the Tao\">By becoming one with the cosmos, one finds the Tao</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One must discard knowledge\">One must discard knowledge</a></li></ul><p><strong>Neo-Taoism: Rationalism &amp; Sentimentalism</strong></p><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Political institutions must constantly change in order to meet the needs of the times\">Political institutions must constantly change in order to meet the needs of the times</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One should act according to one’s natural abilities\">One should act according to one’s natural abilities</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Morality is relative\">Morality is relative</a></li></ul><h5>School of Names</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All things are one and the same\">All things are one and the same</a></li></ul><h5>Legalism</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Legalist school taught the theory and method of organization and leadership, not jurisprudence\">The Legalist school taught the theory and method of organization and leadership, not jurisprudence</a></li><li>Han Feizi: <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately evil\">All humans are innately evil</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A ruler must be able to choose competent subordinates\">A ruler must be able to choose competent subordinates</a></li></ul><h5>Buddhism</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All things in the universe are manifestations of the mind and thus illusory and impermanent\">All things in the universe are manifestations of the mind and thus illusory and impermanent</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Buddhahood is achieved via Sudden Enlightenment\">Buddhahood is achieved via Sudden Enlightenment</a></li></ul><p><strong>Zen</strong></p><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One must discard knowledge\">One must discard knowledge</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Once enlightenment is achieved, the sage returns to normal life\">Once enlightenment is achieved, the sage returns to normal life</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Utilitarianism (book)\">Utilitarianism (book)</a><ul><li>Some of the arguments, specifically ones made by the Mohists, bare a resemblance to utilitarian arguments.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nicomachean Ethics\">Nicomachean Ethics</a><ul><li>Confucian ethics is similar in some way to Aristotelian ethics as both have a focus on virtue.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Theory of Justice\">A Theory of Justice</a><ul><li>This work likewise touches on issues of what is just and what is not.</li></ul></li></ul>","A Theory of Justice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One of the foundational works in politics philosophy in contemporary philosophy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is crucial for understanding the view of justice that is developed/reference by many contemporary academics.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><h5>The Original Position &amp; Methodology</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice has lexical priority over other societal concerns\">Justice has lexical priority over other societal concerns</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"While most people share a common set of fundamental beliefs regarding justice, more broadly, conceptions differ\">While most people share a common set of fundamental beliefs regarding justice, more broadly, conceptions differ</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness is specifically designed for a closed, well-ordered society\">Justice as fairness is specifically designed for a closed, well-ordered society</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness would be accepted by free, rational, and prudential persons\">Justice as fairness would be accepted by free, rational, and prudential persons</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The theory of justice is in large part the theory of rational choice\">The theory of justice is in large part the theory of rational choice</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A good conception of justice is one that aligns with our intuitions and also structures more controversial moral topics\">A good conception of justice is one that aligns with our intuitions and also structures more controversial moral topics</a></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Rawls' Reflective Equilibrium</span></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical\">We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The priority problem has several solutions\">The priority problem has several solutions</a></li></ul><h5>The Two Principles of Justice</h5><p></p><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Only realized institutions can be judged to be just or unjust\">Only realized institutions can be judged to be just or unjust</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A principle must satisfy generality, universality, publicity, ordering, and finality in order to be a candidate principle in the original position\">A principle must satisfy generality, universality, publicity, ordering, and finality in order to be a candidate principle in the original position</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice and injustice can manifest in-whole, discretely, or through sum-threshold criteria\">Justice and injustice can manifest in-whole, discretely, or through sum-threshold criteria</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others\">Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity\">Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness does not apply to individual dealings with specific people, only to institutions\">Justice as fairness does not apply to individual dealings with specific people, only to institutions</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies\">Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The principle of efficiency does not mitigate natural contingencies\">The principle of efficiency does not mitigate natural contingencies</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle requires that expectations be close-knit and chain-connected\">The difference principle requires that expectations be close-knit and chain-connected</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pure procedural justice is the most practical form of justice to evaluate the basic structure with\">Pure procedural justice is the most practical form of justice to evaluate the basic structure with</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fair equality of opportunity is a precondition for pure procedural justice\">Fair equality of opportunity is a precondition for pure procedural justice</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fair equality of opportunity is lexically before the difference principle\">Fair equality of opportunity is lexically before the difference principle</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism\">The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances\">What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The expectations of representative men are defined by the index of primary goods available to them\">The expectations of representative men are defined by the index of primary goods available to them</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"There are two considerations relevant to social positions, which are the existence of equal citizenship, and where someone is in the distribution of wealth\">There are two considerations relevant to social positions, which are the existence of equal citizenship, and where someone is in the distribution of wealth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The basic structure is not responsible for inequalities in associations which one enters voluntarily\">The basic structure is not responsible for inequalities in associations which one enters voluntarily</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle embodies the idea that natural contingencies are commonly shared between members of a society\">The difference principle embodies the idea that natural contingencies are commonly shared between members of a society</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle expresses reciprocity\">The difference principle expresses reciprocity</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle expresses fraternity\">The difference principle expresses fraternity</a></li></ul><h5>Arguments for Justice as Fairness</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rational agents would be risk-averse in the original position\">Rational agents would be risk-averse in the original position</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A society where the worst off are extremely sensitive to their circumstances at the expense of the best off is impossible\">A society where the worst off are extremely sensitive to their circumstances at the expense of the best off is impossible</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Average utilitarianism is more justifiable for rational agents than classical utilitarianism\">Average utilitarianism is more justifiable for rational agents than classical utilitarianism</a> </li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The principle of insufficient reason is false\">The principle of insufficient reason is false</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness fosters self-respect and societal harmony\">Justice as fairness fosters self-respect and societal harmony</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Classical utilitarianism is too demanding\">Classical utilitarianism is too demanding</a></li></ul><h5>Liberty &amp; Political Application</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The intolerant must mostly be tolerated\">The intolerant must mostly be tolerated</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Liberty can only be restricted in order to protect or strengthen the overall system of equal basic liberties\">Liberty can only be restricted in order to protect or strengthen the overall system of equal basic liberties</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Paternalistic decisions are to be guided by an individual’s settled preferences\">Paternalistic decisions are to be guided by an individual’s settled preferences</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People are not apportioned a greater share of liberties beyond a certain limit of their capacity for justice\">People are not apportioned a greater share of liberties beyond a certain limit of their capacity for justice</a></li></ul><h5>Theory of the Good &amp; Stability</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Moral principles originate from rational choice\">Moral principles originate from rational choice</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Thin Theory of the Good serves as a standard framework for agents to consider in the original position\">The Thin Theory of the Good serves as a standard framework for agents to consider in the original position</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Self-respect is the most important primary good\">Self-respect is the most important primary good</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Excellencies are positive externalities\">Excellencies are positive externalities</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Shame can be natural or moral\">Shame can be natural or moral</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The right is not the same as the good\">The right is not the same as the good</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Having a moral personality is sufficient for the full protections of justice\">Having a moral personality is sufficient for the full protections of justice</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a Rawlsian society, people would be educated for autonomy\">In a Rawlsian society, people would be educated for autonomy</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Human social relations are non-trivial\">Human social relations are non-trivial</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Dominant-end moral theories fail\">Dominant-end moral theories fail</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The congruence problem is easier solved by contract theories rather than utilitarian ones\">The congruence problem is easier solved by contract theories rather than utilitarian ones</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A contract theory must be justified, not merely proved\">A contract theory must be justified, not merely proved</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nicomachean Ethics\">Nicomachean Ethics</a><ul><li>These two books overlap substantially on ethics and justice. Rawls' thin theory of the good is an explicit evolution of Aristotle's account, and several atomic notes (e.g., <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances\">What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances</a>) draw the connection directly.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meritocracy\">Meritocracy</a><ul><li>Contains a dedicated section on Rawls' four critiques of meritocracy (no desert, impracticability of tracking merit, incompatibility with public reason, insufficiently democratic). Connects directly to <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies\">Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies</a> and <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The principle of efficiency does not mitigate natural contingencies\">The principle of efficiency does not mitigate natural contingencies</a>.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice and the Meritocratic State\">Justice and the Meritocratic State</a><ul><li>Mulligan's defense of desert directly targets Rawls' no-desert argument. His response — that genetics are metaphysically essential to a person and thus a valid desert base — is the most developed reply to <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle embodies the idea that natural contingencies are commonly shared between members of a society\">The difference principle embodies the idea that natural contingencies are commonly shared between members of a society</a>.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice, Equal Opportunity, and the Family\">Justice, Equal Opportunity, and the Family</a><ul><li>Fishkin explicitly draws on Rawls' pure procedural justice. His trilemma (Merit + Equality of Life Chances + Family Autonomy) exposes structural limits of <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fair equality of opportunity is a precondition for pure procedural justice\">Fair equality of opportunity is a precondition for pure procedural justice</a> when family autonomy is taken seriously.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Diachronic Rejection of Meritocracy\">A Diachronic Rejection of Meritocracy</a><ul><li>Builds directly on Rawls' distinction between natural and social contingencies, and on pure procedural justice, to argue that desert-based meritocracy is self-defeating in liberal societies.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capitalism and Freedom\">Capitalism and Freedom</a><ul><li>Friedman's thesis that economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom stands in direct tension with Rawls' lexical priority of equal basic liberties over economic gains (see <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fair equality of opportunity is lexically before the difference principle\">Fair equality of opportunity is lexically before the difference principle</a>).</li></ul></li><li><em>Defending the One Percent</em><ul><li>Mankiw explicitly attacks the Rawlsian veil of ignorance, arguing it generates unintuitive conclusions (e.g., mandatory organ donation). Also contests the empirical premise that income inequality is primarily rent-seeking rather than talent-driven.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Free Will</span><ul><li>Rawls' claim that natural talents are \"morally arbitrary\" implicitly presupposes that agents bear no moral responsibility for unchosen traits — a claim that sits at the intersection of moral luck and compatibilism debates covered here.</li></ul></li></ul>","All ethical theories are fundamentally teleological":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>All ethical theories, despite their justification, are ultimately justified by their consequence (their teleology).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is what Mill contends, and one of his supporting arguments for utilitarianism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Even though theories might claim that their ethical principles are derived out of some first principles unrelated to the outcome of those principles, ultimately, it is the outcome of those principles that lead the first principles to be established in the first place.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Utilitarianism fails\">Utilitarianism fails</a><ul><li>This is a potential counterargument to this idea.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Theory of Justice\">A Theory of Justice</a><ul><li>Fundamentally, the purpose of this politcal/ethical system is to maximize some desired end.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nicomachean Ethics\">Nicomachean Ethics</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Cavalry were effective in ancient warfare because of the mobility asymmetry that they created":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Cavalry were really powerful in ancient warfare because of their hypermobility.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that they were able to more easily take advantage of weaknesses in an enemy formation, and exploit these weaknesses in a timely fashion.</p><h4>References</h4>","Every action has an end goal":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Every action has an end goal, that is, it’s final cause. There are things which are means to an end, and there are also things that are an end in and of themselves.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the fundamental premise that underlies Aristotle’s arguments in Book I of the <em>Ethics</em>, since if all things have an end goal, it must be the case that humans have one too.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>It seems that things which is an end in and of itself is happiness, or manifestations of happiness is some fashion. It seems that the fact of “all things having an end goal” can only be established through an inductive argument, whereby all things that we observe have an end goal, so, everything must have an end goal.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated\">Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated</a><ul><li>Aristotle’s two forms of goodness.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation\">Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation</a><ul><li>There are three potential definitions of happiness for Aristotle, although only one of them, goodness, and the action thereof, Aristotle considers the true definition of happiness.</li></ul></li></ul>","Meritocracy can be justified either consequentially or categorically, and the two justifications diverge":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One may favour merit-based allocation because it produces good results, in which case its value is contingent, or because allocating by merit is intrinsically right, in which case one must accept it even when the results are worse.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The two justifications are usually run together, and separating them shows that most defenders of meritocracy cannot have both.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The consequentialist is vulnerable to the case where some non-merit factor produces the better outcome, and must then abandon merit. The categorical defender is vulnerable to the case where merit-based allocation predictably underperforms, and must accept the loss.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Merit is contextual and internal, so it cannot be read off outcomes\">Merit is contextual and internal, so it cannot be read off outcomes</a><ul><li>Sharpens the problem for the consequentialist, who needs merit to be measurable.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Matching people to roles by merit may be less efficient than letting them choose\">Matching people to roles by merit may be less efficient than letting them choose</a><ul><li>The efficiency case that the consequentialist justification has to survive.</li></ul></li></ul>","Only an absolute monarch can keep people out of a ‘state of nature’":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that an absolute monarch is needed in order to people moral and prevent them from degrading into anarchy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This line of thought has been advocated for by a variety of thinkers, including Thomas Hobbes and <em>Mozi</em>.</p><h4>Mohism</h4><p>An absolute monarch is needed in order to maintain the order in the world and prevent a state of complete anarchy. They are needed to propagate and enforce the doctrine of all-embracing love. The entire purpose of the state, in fact, is to “unify the standards (一同国之义),” or police the morality of the nation and ensure that it remains homogenous. The legitimacy of any such ruler is necessarily born out of both the will of the people and the Will of God.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Everyone in the world should love everyone else without discrimination\">Everyone in the world should love everyone else without discrimination</a><ul><li>This is the principle that the absolute monarch would enforce.</li></ul></li></ul>","Phospholipids are the main building block of cell membranes":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Phospholipids are the main building block of cell membranes because they are amphipathic, with their hydrophilic heads facing outside a cell, and the hydrophobic side remaining on the inside.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Phospholipids are an important part of AP Bio, and their purpose is important for cells to function.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Unlike triglycerides, phospholipids only have <strong>two</strong> fatty acid chains.</p><h4>References</h4>","Self-respect is the most important primary good":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rawls argues that the most important primary good is self-respect. Self-respect means that one is confident that one’s conception of the good is worth carrying out, and that one has the capability to carry out that rational plan.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Self-respect is an important aspect of Justice as Fairness. Since it is the most important primary good, our political systems should be designed to promote it.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Self-respect has two components:</p><ol><li>One’s own conviction that his conception of the good is worth carrying out.</li><li>A confidence in one’s ability to fulfill one’s intentions.</li></ol><p>The absolute achievement of something that is spelled out in our rational plans is unimportant, with the more important thing being our sense of worth as the actor of the achievement.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The expectations of representative men are defined by the index of primary goods available to them\">The expectations of representative men are defined by the index of primary goods available to them</a><ul><li>So then it is most important to index the level of self-respect.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness fosters self-respect and societal harmony\">Justice as fairness fosters self-respect and societal harmony</a><ul><li>Gives the mechanism by which a well-ordered society produces the self-respect that this note claims is most important.</li></ul></li></ul>","Suffering can serve as a vehicle for moral superiority":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Suffering is a way for some people to feel a sense of moral superiority.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common theme in various philosophies or literary works.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In <em>The Brothers Karamazov</em>, this manifests in:</p><ul><li>Father Ferapont, who lives as an aesthetic in the woods. In doing so, he claims that he is able to “see” demons, and he becomes revered by people after the death of Zossima, since he denounces Zossima after his death. This is likely because he experienced suffering while living in the woods.</li><li>Katerina Ivanova continues to express her own suffering as a result of being unable to love Ivan because he is engaged to Dimitri, although she is able to end her affair with Dimitri at any time she wants.</li></ul><p>This is the idea of “lacerations” that is present throughout the novel. Put another way, it is the ego, disguised as the victim.</p><ul><li>People can feed on a feeling of hopelessness are vindictiveness because it makes them feel special, or morally superior to others.</li><li>At the end of this, the only possible result of isolation. One is not able to relate to the pain of others, since we are so stuck in our own pain.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Sweden practices \"big-state libertarianism\"":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While there is typically a dichotomy between have a big state and libertarianism, it seems that Sweden was able to preserve the features that matter the most from both systems.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows that it would potentially be possible to mix libertarian ideals with a big state.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are strong social safety nets in Sweden and high taxes. At the same time, much of the public services are provided by the private sector, there are no inherence taxes, it is easy to hire and fire, and there is no national minimum wage. There are more billionaires per-person in Sweden than in the US. It seems that Sweden was able to create a large, impersonal state, a state which frees individuals from traditional family bonds and other sort of societal bonds, creating both something that is akin to the ideal of the libertarian while also enacting policies that the left would support.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end\">Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end</a><ul><li>It seems that Sweden’s big government might still suffer from this though.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Government should not intervene in the affairs of private individuals in most cases\">Government should not intervene in the affairs of private individuals in most cases</a><ul><li>Sweden does this too a certain degree, and in certain realms of life.</li></ul></li></ul>","That history entails progress is a modern conception":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Lowith argues that this is the standard modern conception of history; i.e., conceiving of history as a progressive force that gradually eliminates parts of the problem of evil until the problem is no more.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Lowith argues that this attitude is uniquely modern, and differs both from the Classical and Christian solutions to the problem of evil.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>During antiquity, people dealt with the problem of evil with rebellion, as can be seen through the myth of Prometheus. The other response is subservience and submission to the higher authority, as to Christ. The Greek believed that man has the resources to be able to meet every situation, and as thus, they were not as concerned with the meaning of history, but rather with the operation of the cosmos.</p><ul><li>They were humanists.</li><li>Since human nature does not change, events that have occurred in the past will occur again. In this way, history is deterministic in a very rough sense, thus explaining their lack of interest in the philosophy of history.</li></ul><p>For the Christians and Jews, however, history is primarily a history of salvation.</p><ul><li>Believed that knowledge of the future is something that is predetermined by God, though there is personal will involved. Either way, man is not subject to knowledge of the future, as the Greeks believed.</li><li>This is best exemplified by the thought of Augustine, who believed that history is defined by the two dispensations: the death of Christ and the second coming of Christ. Additionally, he believed that providence was decided by God and carried out by him.<ul><li>The only visible sign of divine providence is the death of Christ. If the signs were too obvious otherwise, then we would lose our ability to have faith in God.</li></ul></li></ul><p>Our modern conception of history is Christian in framework but un-Christian in substance: we believe in progress towards some future “outside of history,” while dropping the fact that the death of Christ is the beginning of the end and that his life and death are what imbues history with this meaning.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Meaning in History</em>, p. 202</p><p>Neither of these two interpretations, however, explains the curious mixture of <strong>our “Christian world,” which lives by the hope in a better world and yet sets its hope on material production and welfare.</strong></p></blockquote><h4>Note</h4><p>It seems that the idea of ancient Greece having an attitude of rebellion is a mistaken once, since their general attitude is likewise one of submission to the gods. This can be seen through the histories written by Thucydides and Herodotus and Polybius. This is in fact one of the common points between ancient Greek and Christian thought: they both submitted to fate, unlike today, where we look towards progress. Nonetheless, there is truth in the fact that they believed that since human nature remained constant, the future was in some way, predictable.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Vico’s views on history</p><p>In many ways, Vico’s view of history is rather classical instead of Christian. He adopts a theory that is similar to the cyclical theory of the ancients. This, however, puts him in conflict with the progressive theory of the Christians. This is illustrated by the following quote:</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Meaning in History</em>, p. 135</p><p>History as seen by Vico has a prehistoric beginning by no end and fulfilment, and yet it is ruled by providence for the sake of mankind.</p></blockquote></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As history progresses, morality advances as well\">As history progresses, morality advances as well</a><ul><li>Specifically, this is the modern idea that morality advances through history.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority\">Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority</a><ul><li>This is the “submissiveness” that is referred to in this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a><ul><li>It seems that the conception here of Classical views on the problem of evil may be too simple? Oedipus rebels against his fate, but he fails. Other classical works like the Odyssey also describe a process of rebellion against the gods but the ultimate failure of such attempts. </li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Rise of the Roman Empire (book)\">The Rise of the Roman Empire (book)</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus at Colonus\">Oedipus at Colonus</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"History can be written as an evidential inquiry rather than as a record of divine causes\">History can be written as an evidential inquiry rather than as a record of divine causes</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Oedipus complex appears in many works of literature":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The pattern Freud named the Oedipus complex, in which a child’s attachment to one parent is coupled with hostility toward the other, recurs across literature written long before the theory existed, which is what makes psychoanalysis usable as an interpretive lens rather than merely as a clinical one.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>If the pattern predates its description, then either it is a real regularity of family life that authors observed independently, or it is loose enough to be read into anything, and which of those holds determines whether psychoanalytic criticism has any evidential value.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>Hamlet’s solicitude toward his mother and hatred of his uncle fit the pattern directly, and Ophelia’s relationship with her father is a second instance within the same play.</li><li>Read this way, Hamlet’s repression of his own thoughts explains his inaction, which is otherwise the hardest feature of the play to motivate.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The pursuit of complete certainty delays action past the point where it can succeed\">The pursuit of complete certainty delays action past the point where it can succeed</a><ul><li>A competing explanation of the same inaction, which does not require the psychoanalytic apparatus.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Sound and the Fury":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Tells the story of the Compsons as they descend from prosperity to despair in the American South.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>One of the great American works.</p><h4>Themes</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People often obsess over abstractions\">People often obsess over abstractions</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Old South was reluctant to change after the Civil War\">The Old South was reluctant to change after the Civil War</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One ought to focus on the present and not remain bogged down in the past\">One ought to focus on the present and not remain bogged down in the past</a></li></ul><h4>Characters</h4><p><strong>Benjamin Compson</strong></p><ul><li>Severely mentally disabled, only able to moan, scream, and cry. Unable to speak.</li><li>Views the world through associative memory rather than linearly through time.</li><li>Views the world of the Compsons as “good” and the outside world as “bad.”<ul><li>Is only able to see the outside world through Caddy.</li></ul></li><li>He interprets engagement with the outside world as “muddying,” and is unable to accept it when it occurs.</li><li>Benjamin is unable to understand the adult world, specifically the idea of cultivating one’s personal appearance and understanding one’s place in the world among others.<ul><li>He doesn’t like it when Caddy smells of perfume (something that covers up her natural smell) and instead prefers that she smell like trees.</li><li>He constantly moans in the presence of others, despite people’s dislike of such behavior.</li></ul></li><li>Benjy is unrealiable because he<ul><li>Has an inventive to portray the people that he likes in a better light that they actually might be</li><li>And he is unable to mentally comprehend everything that is going on.</li></ul></li><li>\"Idiot\" is a formal developmental term that categorizes someone as being stuck at the mental development of age 2.</li><li>The sense of smell is important in the book, because while Freud said that as civilization developed, smell has been replaced by sight, Faulkner disagrees.<ul><li>Through the character of Benji Faulkner portrays the importance of smell in our understanding of the world, even if at a base level.</li></ul></li><li>Benji demands that Caddy stay with him, physically and mentally (developmentally).</li><li>But this is an impossible demand. <ul><li>Caddy starts wearing perfume after she reaches adolescence.</li><li>Caddy is caught kissing a boy.</li></ul></li><li>Benji is essentially holding Caddy's development hostage.</li><li>Caddy losing her virginity is the breaking point for Benji, since that is not something that Caddy can wash away. It is permanent.</li><li>Benji demands that time stands still. He wants the world the freeze with him. He represents the past.</li></ul><p><strong>Candance Compson</strong></p><ul><li>Caring and represents the point of intersection between the Compson world and the outside world.</li><li>She is the conduit whereby the Compson boys are able to view the world beyond just their home (through the contrast between her innocence and her “spoiling”).</li></ul><p><strong>Quentin Compson</strong></p><ul><li>The fact that he focuses on his shadow is because he wants to focus on the passage of time, since that is something that is important to him.</li><li>A martyr for the cause of purity.<ul><li>Not a hero</li></ul></li><li>Quentin kills himself in order to freeze his grief (at the loss of innocence of Caddy) in time.<ul><li>He breaks his watch.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Notes</h4><ul><li>Each of the women represents something:<ul><li>Ms compson: the past</li><li>Miss Quentin &amp; Caddy: the present</li><li>Dilsey: the future</li></ul></li><li>In this way, each of the female characters has a match with the male characters.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a><ul><li>Both works deal with themes of fate and time.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hamlet (play)\">Hamlet (play)</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Traditionalism, or a lack of desire to accumulate capital, is capitalist spirit’s greatest enemy. It is in essence a rejection of the entrepreneurial spirit and in general a rejection of the desire to improve. It is also a rejection of the divine calling that Weber attributes for the genesis of the capitalist spirit.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This concept is used to contrast the capitalist spirt with the “status quo\" at the time, which Weber analyzed as being traditionalism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian\">The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian</a><ul><li>Traditionalism is a rejection of exactly this calling, which is why the two are opposed.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic compulsion is detached from religious calling beginning during the Enlightenment\">Economic compulsion is detached from religious calling beginning during the Enlightenment</a><ul><li>If the religious motive drops away, it is unclear what remains to hold traditionalism off.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling\">The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling</a></li></ul>","The decline in the labor share of capital is related to a decline in construction productivity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The decline in the labor share of income can be partly attributed to the decline inn construction productivity and the rise in real housing prices (the two are complements).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This worsens inequality and stunts economics growth.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Due to the stagnant productivity in the real estate market, the real price of buildings have risen. Since workers have to be housed in buildings, this means that the labor share of income would fall.</li><li>The Baumol effect is also at play, since while manufacturing productivity has skyrocketed, construction productivity has not. </li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Baumol&#x27;s Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries\">Baumol's Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries</a><ul><li>This effect is at play in the case of this problem described herein.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy\">Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy</a><ul><li>This principle is given evidence by this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","The end of the Cold War marked a major change in global security":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The end of the cold war produced many different effect, a major one of which is the decline of the military power of the West and associated problems.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This leaves room for other powers, specifically in East Asia and the Middle East, to rise to regional prominence.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Specifically, Huntington outlines five observations:</p><ol><li>The collapse of Russian military capabilities.</li><li>Decline of Western military power in general (decreased spending, etc.)</li><li>Increase in East Asian spending on military.</li><li>Military capabilities are becoming more eventually distributed across borders.</li><li>Security is increasingly being regionalized, such that future conflicts will be largely regional, not directly between superpowers.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Liberal democracy may be the endpoint of ideological development\">Liberal democracy may be the endpoint of ideological development</a><ul><li>Here is some evidence that could be used to undermine this claim.</li></ul></li></ul>","The fall of the Roman Republic could be partly attributed to the fact that the Senate failed to adequately represent the Plebeians":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One potential cause for the fall of the Roman Republic was that it failed to appropriately account for the interests of the Plebians, which led to the Plebian empowerment of rouge demagogues, which eventually lead to the downfall of the Roman Republic.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one potential cause of the fall of the Republic.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>I think that the direction of causation of unclear, i.e., whether it is the demagogues which enflamed the passions of the masses, or whether they merely took advantage of the circumstances and, in the name of the good of the masses, engaged in their reckless behavior.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Roman Republican political system is incredibly stable due to its composite construction\">The Roman Republican political system is incredibly stable due to its composite construction</a><ul><li>It seems that Polybius’s prediction were actually right, as it pertains to the interpretation of the fall of the Republic here, since towards the tail end of the Republic, the people gained more and more power and eventually there was mass chaos, until the reinstitution of a dictator in the form of Augustus.</li></ul></li></ul>","The goal of the city is to foster virtue":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The teleology of the city is one directed towards the cultivation of virtue. All parts of the city function imbued with virtue, and producing virtue.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a fundamental character of cities for Aristotle.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>If a city does not have virtue as its common goal, then it is no longer a city. And by the “city,” that references the leaders of the city and all its citizens, who must all live according to political virtue and personal virtue, which, in a virtuous city, become one and the same. <strong>Most importantly, for a city to be virtuous is for it to serve the common interest.</strong></p><ul><li>When cities deviate from the virtuous path, they justify the deviance via a particular criterion of desert, such as wealth (oligarchy) or freedom/equality (democracy).</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends\">Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends</a><ul><li>One important function of the city is that of educating the populace and making them virtuous.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue\">The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue</a><ul><li>Only the leaders need the virtue of prudence in a city.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law\">Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law</a><ul><li>Justice is important for the city too.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A contract theory must be justified, not merely proved\">A contract theory must be justified, not merely proved</a><ul><li>This would be a critique that Aristotle levels at Contractarianism.</li></ul></li></ul>","The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that divine calls are a concept coherent to Judeo-Christianity, and as especially prevalent with some branches of Judeo-Christianity.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This support’s Weber’s thesis regarding the origins of the capitalist spirit from Protestantism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The elimination of salvation through the Church and the sacraments separates Calvinism from Catholicism\">The elimination of salvation through the Church and the sacraments separates Calvinism from Catholicism</a><ul><li>The doctrinal move that gives the calling its economic force.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic compulsion is detached from religious calling beginning during the Enlightenment\">Economic compulsion is detached from religious calling beginning during the Enlightenment</a><ul><li>Where this idea eventually goes: the calling is discarded but the compulsion remains.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling\">The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism\">The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism</a></li></ul>","The ontological argument is not convincing":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>I think that there are many valid critiques against Anselm’s Ontological argument. There are some well-known ones that are not included, here, as I simply offer my own perspective in the Zettel.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Anselm’s Ontological Argument is a commonly brought up argument for the existence of God. Here, I offer my own critique of it.</p><h4>My Critique</h4><p>Anselm seems to create artificial ontological stratums between things that “exist in imagination” and “things that ‘exist’.” I think this is a little questionable. Anselm’s argument that the “greatest” possible being that we can conceive, ceteris paribus, must exist not only in the imagination, but also actually <em>exist.</em> However, this seems a little questionable. For example, take an idea such as “culture.” It is simply a type of concept that could not make sense to exist in a physical sense. Ceteris paribus, would a “culture” that exist be better than a “culture” that exists only in the imagination? While this does not disprove Anselm’s argument, it makes it more likely that Anselm is wrong.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Radical doubt can be used to clear the ground for a reconstructed epistemology\">Radical doubt can be used to clear the ground for a reconstructed epistemology</a></li></ul>","There is a structural gap between Chinese and US AI capabilities":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There exists a fundamental structural gap between the AI capabilities of the US and China, due to the different ways in which the capital market functions in China vs the US, and the availability of chips, etc.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This would shape American foreign policy and the way that the US views Chinese AI development.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Chinese models gain a lot from distilling American AI, so as more companies prevent their best models from getting public access, Chinese models will remain behind. Also, Chinese capital markets demand early monetization, so there is no funding for long-term traditional R&amp;D. There is also a chip performance gap.</li><li>All of this is to suggest that the US-China gap is structural rather than temporary.</li></ul><h4>Note</h4><p>What China has on its side, however, is:</p><ul><li>Cheap energy,</li><li>Willingness to give inexperienced engineers chances,</li><li>Institutionalized self-criticism,</li><li>A willingness to copy from the US.</li></ul><p>These factors will probably prevent the US from pulling too far ahead of China.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interoperability in US software benefits US AI development\">Interoperability in US software benefits US AI development</a><ul><li>This is part of the US advantage too.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"There is fierce domestic competition between Chinese AI firms\">There is fierce domestic competition between Chinese AI firms</a><ul><li>This destroys the narrative of a united “Chinese AI policy,” and is relevant for investigating the development of AI in China.</li></ul></li></ul>","To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean, which means not acting with deficiency nor with excess. Additionally, to be virtuous means to be in the condition of being in the mean, so the mean cannot simply be a passing impulse.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of Aristotle’s most significant moral ideas, and the central pillar of his moral philosophy.</p><h4>Aristotelianism</h4><p>The mean is something that is circumstantial and not absolute. In other words, it is ordinal, not cardinal. It varies based on the time, location, people involved, etc. In some things, the mean is the “extreme.” this has two meanings:</p><ol><li>The very fact that some action is virtuous means that it is extreme on the “virtuous-immoral” spectrum, given that is the most virtuous things that you can do.</li><li>Additionally, there are some things that are inherently bad, and there is no “mean” to reach for those things, including spite, envy, adultery, theft, murder, etc.</li></ol><h4>Chinese Philosophy</h4><p>An analogous idea is found in Chinese philosophy. As both stated in the <em>Book of Changes</em> (易经) and by Laozi, when things develop to one extreme, they are then brought to the other extreme. Therefore, one must seek the mean, the balance between the two extremes.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>Aristotle stresses, however, that virtue must be repeatedly practiced, and not simply the result of a passing impulse.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a><ul><li>The quality of virtuousness referenced in this note is referring to its adherence to the principle of the golden mean.</li></ul></li></ul>","We must tolerate, even celebrate eccentric people in society":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Since it is the case that we tolerate people having different aesthetic choices and the such, it seems that, we must also tolerate people with different lifestyle choices, i.e., the eccentric.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This must be the case since otherwise, we miss out on the geniuses in society and the people who are truly of strong character.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality\">‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality</a><ul><li>Suppressing individuality is bad.</li></ul></li></ul>","We often mistake our own unclearness of thought for some inherent mystery in the subject of our thought":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It is often the case that our own unclearness of thought regarding a subject is projected unto our understanding of the subject itself, such that we belief it to be an inherently mysterious subject.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This diagnoses a significant problem in our everyday epistemology.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>When it comes to discussing complex topics such as the nature of morality or the meaning of life, we often come to the conclusion that there is ultimately just no inherent solution or some equivalent conclusion. However, we must never discount the possibility that it is merely that we have not thought long enough or have not clarified our thoughts enough.</p><h4>References</h4>","Weak and Kafkaesque institutions created a large informal economy in Latin America":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The economies of Latin American often have large informal sectors due to the fact that they have weak institutions and extremely bureaucratic legal systems.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the large informal sector that exists in the economies of Latin America.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Latin Americans create informal businesses because of the high cost of legalization.<ul><li>But they do pay taxes, since most of the tax is in the form of excise and consumption taxes.</li><li>They also pay for this by holding large cash balances, whose value is constantly decreasing due to the deficit spending by the government and the inflation that comes with it.</li><li>In fact, they have their own informal set of system, which, although is not as strong as the government enforced rules, respects property rights, insurance systems, and torts.</li></ul></li><li>Both homeowners do not have titles, which means that it is difficult for them to use it as a collateral for loans.</li><li>Since the laws are weak, there is no limited liability.</li><li>There is no enforcement of contracts, which means that it is difficult to pool resources effectively, since people can just betray that people that they are working with whenever they want.<ul><li>This means that overall production will be less efficient.</li></ul></li><li>Bad laws are created (and a lot of them) because most of it originated form the executive.<ul><li>When laws are not just created by the executive, it is not monopolized by one person/office.</li><li>There is no way for the people who create the laws to be accountable in Latin America.</li><li>There is no public access to information/public accountability.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit\">A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit</a><ul><li>This is the case in Latin America, leading to the development of a large informal sector.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trust measurement for the quality of institutions\">Trust measurement for the quality of institutions</a><ul><li>There is no trust in the institutions that exist in many Latin American countries.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society\">Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society</a><ul><li>The information in this note could both be used to argue against and for this note.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
